{{!--
  What something is, opened by holding it.

  A panel of Phonedry's own rather than dnd5e's item sheet, which is a desktop
  application with tabs — and one of the windows that renders badly in WebKit.

  CONSTRAINT: exactly one top-level element. See the note in header.hbs.
--}}
{{!-- The dimmed area above the panel dismisses it.

      The close button is at the top of a panel anchored to the bottom of the
      screen, so on a tall phone it sits in the middle — and reaching past it to
      tap the dimmed area is what a thumb does first. That is the standard sheet
      gesture on both platforms, so the instinct is right and the panel should
      honour it rather than ignore the tap. The button stays for anyone who
      wants it, and because a backdrop tap is unmarked. --}}
<div class="phonedry-describe" data-action="closeDescription"
     {{#unless describe}}hidden{{/unless}}>
  {{#if describe}}
    <div class="phonedry-describe__panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
         aria-label="{{describe.name}}">
      <div class="phonedry-describe__head">
        {{!-- A rules page has no artwork of its own, and an <img> with an empty
              src resolves against the page URL and draws a broken icon. --}}
        {{#if describe.img}}
          <img class="phonedry-describe__icon" src="{{describe.img}}" alt="" />
        {{/if}}
        <span class="phonedry-describe__titles">
          <span class="phonedry-describe__name">{{describe.name}}</span>
          {{#if describe.subtitle}}
            <span class="phonedry-describe__subtitle">{{describe.subtitle}}</span>
          {{/if}}
        </span>
        {{!-- One control, whose meaning follows the state.

              Following a link replaces what the panel is showing, so while
              there is something behind it this goes back rather than dismissing
              — otherwise reading a cited rule costs you the rule you were
              reading it from. At the root there is nothing to go back to, so it
              closes. Two separate controls would overlap and leave a player
              guessing which one loses their place. --}}
        <button type="button" class="phonedry-describe__close" data-action="closeDescription"
                aria-label="{{#if describeBack}}{{localize 'PHONEDRY.Describe.Back'}}{{else}}{{localize 'PHONEDRY.Describe.Close'}}{{/if}}">
          {{#if describeBack}}
            <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-left" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          {{else}}
            &times;
          {{/if}}
        </button>
      </div>

      {{!-- What it costs, how far it reaches, how long it lasts.

            Above the description because these are the questions asked before
            reading a word of it, and answering them used to mean reading all of
            it. Every value is dnd5e's own composed string, so nothing here is
            derived a second time. --}}
      {{#if describe.facts}}
        <dl class="phonedry-describe__facts">
          {{#each describe.facts}}
            <div class="phonedry-describe__fact">
              <dt>{{localize this.label}}</dt>
              <dd>{{this.value}}</dd>
            </div>
          {{/each}}
        </dl>
      {{/if}}

      {{!-- Enriched by Foundry before it reaches here, so inline rolls and
            references read as text rather than as markup.

            Plenty of items carry no description at all — dnd5e ships chain mail
            and a mace blank — and an empty box reads as the panel having failed
            rather than as there being nothing to say. The facts above are then
            the whole of the answer, so this says so and gets out of the way. --}}
      {{#if describe.hasText}}
        <div class="phonedry-describe__body">{{{describe.html}}}</div>
      {{else}}
        <p class="phonedry-describe__body phonedry-describe__body--empty">
          {{localize "PHONEDRY.Describe.NoText"}}
        </p>
      {{/if}}
    </div>
  {{/if}}
</div>
